/* Rank mode · JEE Mains Physics · Electrostatics. Same page as the other chapters; no past-year questions yet.
   Loaded with the JEE pack. Plain data only; the diagrams (dipole, gaussE, shellV, capDiel, capNet, share) are drawn by rank-figs.js. Nothing leaves the device.
   F(section, level, name, formulas, how to apply, example, diagram): level 1 = must know, 2 = also useful.
   Constants used in the examples: k = 1/(4πε₀) = 9 × 10⁹ N m²/C², ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/N m², e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, g = 10 m/s². */
(() => {
'use strict';
const F=(g,p,n,f,how,ex,fig)=>({g,p,n,f,how,ex,...(fig?{fig}:{})});
window.PhysicaRankElectroJee={
chapter:'Electrostatics',
secs:['Charge and Coulomb’s law','Electric field','Electric dipole','Gauss’s law','Potential and potential energy','Conductors','Capacitance and combinations','Energy and dielectrics','Joining, charging and circuits'],
table:[['Charge q','AT (coulomb, C)'],['Electric field E','MLT⁻³A⁻¹ (N/C = V/m)'],['Electric potential V','ML²T⁻³A⁻¹ (volt)'],['Electric flux','ML³T⁻³A⁻¹ (N m²/C)'],['Permittivity ε₀','M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A² (C²/N m²)'],['Dipole moment p','LTA (C m)'],['Capacitance C','M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A² (farad)'],['Surface charge density σ','L⁻²TA (C/m²)'],['Linear charge density λ','L⁻¹TA (C/m)'],['Energy density','ML⁻¹T⁻² (J/m³)'],['Dielectric constant K','no unit'],['Time constant RC','T (second)']],
formulas:[
F('Charge and Coulomb’s law',1,'Properties of charge','charge is quantised: q = ne, n a whole number, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C (quarks of ±e/3 and ±2e/3 are never free) · charge is conserved in an isolated system and is additive (a scalar, added with sign) · charge does not depend on speed (mass does) · a body gets charged by gaining (negative) or losing (positive) electrons · m(e) = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, m(p) = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg · 1 C = 6.25 × 10¹⁸ electrons','Convert between charge and the number of electrons with n = q ÷ e. Add charges with their signs; when two identical conductors touch, the total charge is shared equally.','A body with 5 × 10¹² extra electrons has q = −5 × 10¹² × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = −8 × 10⁻⁷ C. A charge of 3.2 μC is 3.2 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2 × 10¹³ electrons.'),
F('Charge and Coulomb’s law',1,'Coulomb’s law','F = {kq₁q₂;r²}, k = {1;4πε₀} = 9 × 10⁹ N m²/C² · vector form: F⃗₁₂ = {kq₁q₂;r²} r̂₁₂ (like charges repel, unlike attract) · ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/N m² · in a medium of dielectric constant K: F = {F₀;K} = {q₁q₂;4πε₀Kr²} · valid for point charges at rest; obeys Newton’s third law; central and conservative · electric force is far stronger than gravity: for two electrons F(e) ÷ F(g) = 4.2 × 10⁴²','Put the magnitudes of the charges in the formula for the size, and take the direction from the signs. In a medium divide by K; if the same pair is moved from air to a liquid and the force drops to 1/K, the liquid has constant K.','2 μC and 3 μC at 30 cm in air: F = 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 0.3² = 0.6 N. In water (K = 80) the force is 0.6 ÷ 80 = 7.5 × 10⁻³ N.','coul'),
F('Charge and Coulomb’s law',1,'Superposition and equilibrium of charges on a line','F⃗ = F⃗₁ + F⃗₂ + … (vector sum; the force between two charges is not changed by a third one) · a third charge is in equilibrium on the line joining q₁ and q₂ at x from q₁: same signs: between them, x = {d√q₁;√q₁ + √q₂} · opposite signs: outside the line, on the side of the smaller charge, x = {d√q₁;√q₂ − √q₁} (|q₂| > |q₁|) · all three free: the middle charge is q₀ = −{q₁q₂;(√q₁ + √q₂)²} for outer charges q₁, q₂ of the same sign','Equate the two forces on the third charge (kq₁q/x² = kq₂q/(d − x)²) to get √q₁ ÷ x = √q₂ ÷ (d − x). The position does not depend on the third charge or its sign. For all three to be free, also balance a force on an outer charge.','+4 μC and +9 μC, 30 cm apart: x = 30 × 2 ÷ (2 + 3) = 12 cm from the 4 μC. For all three to be in equilibrium the middle charge is −(4 × 9) ÷ 5² = −1.44 μC.'),
F('Charge and Coulomb’s law',1,'Charged balls on strings and in a liquid','two identical charged balls hang from the same point on strings of length L: tanθ = {F;mg} with F = {kq²;x²} and x = 2L sinθ · small angle: x³ = {2kq²L;mg} = {q²L;2πε₀mg} · same angle in air and in a liquid of density σ (balls of density ρ): F is divided by K and the weight is reduced by buoyancy, so K = {ρ;ρ − σ}','Draw the three forces on a ball (weight, tension, Coulomb force) and use tanθ = F ÷ mg. For a small angle replace tanθ by x ÷ 2L. When the angle is unchanged on immersing, equate the tangents: F ÷ mg in air with (F ÷ K) ÷ (mg − upthrust) in the liquid, which gives K(mg − upthrust) = mg.','q = 10⁻⁸ C on each ball of mass 0.18 g, L = 1 m: x³ = 2 × 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻¹⁶ × 1 ÷ (1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 10) = 10⁻³ m³, so x = 0.10 m. Balls of density 1600 kg/m³ keep the same angle in oil of density 800 kg/m³ when K = 1600 ÷ 800 = 2.','balls'),
F('Charge and Coulomb’s law',2,'Charges at the corners of a square or polygon','equal charges q at the corners of a square of side a: force on one corner = {kq²;a²}(√2 + {1;2}) directed away from the centre · force on a charge at the centre of a regular polygon of equal charges is zero · a charge Q at the centre keeps the system in equilibrium when Q = −{q(1 + 2√2);4} · if one charge of a symmetric set is removed, the force on the centre charge equals that charge’s force, reversed','Resolve the forces of the neighbours along and perpendicular to the diagonal, using the symmetry, and balance the net outward push against the pull of the centre charge, which is a distance a/√2 away.','Four charges of +4 μC at the corners of a square: the central charge needed is Q = −4 × (1 + 2.828) ÷ 4 = −3.83 μC.'),
F('Electric field',1,'Electric field of a point charge','E = {kq;r²} (N/C or V/m) directed away from +q and towards −q · F⃗ = qE⃗ (force on −q is opposite to E⃗) · superposition: E⃗ = ΣE⃗ᵢ, a vector sum · in a medium E = {kq;Kr²} · E = 0 at a point where two equal like charges are at equal distances on opposite sides (the midpoint) · two unequal like charges: the neutral point is between them, at x = d√q₁ ÷ (√q₁ + √q₂) from q₁','Find the size of each field from kq/r², draw its direction, resolve into x and y parts and add. A point of zero field exists only where the fields point in opposite directions.','The field 0.3 m from a 2 nC charge: E = 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.09 = 200 N/C. The force on a 5 μC charge placed there is 200 × 5 × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻³ N.'),
F('Electric field',2,'Two equal charges: field on the bisector','two charges q at (±a, 0): E on the perpendicular bisector at a distance y = {2kqy;(a² + y²)^(3/2)}, directed along y · maximum when y = {a;√2}: E(max) = {4kq;3√3 a²} · for +q and −q the field on the bisector is the equatorial field {2kqa;(a² + y²)^(3/2)}, parallel to the line joining them · at the midpoint of equal like charges E = 0','Add the two fields as vectors; the components along the line cancel by symmetry (like charges) or add (unlike charges). To find the maximum set dE/dy = 0.','q = 2 μC at (±0.1 m, 0): at y = 0.1 m E = 2 × 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.1 ÷ (0.02)^(3/2) = 1.27 × 10⁶ N/C; the maximum is 4 × 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ (3√3 × 0.01) = 1.39 × 10⁶ N/C, at y = 0.0707 m.','bis'),
F('Electric field',2,'Electric field lines','lines start on positive charges and end on negative charges (or go to infinity) · the tangent gives the direction of E⃗; the density of lines is proportional to E · two lines never cross (E has one direction at a point); they never form closed loops in electrostatics · they leave a conductor at right angles · the number of lines is proportional to the charge · a charge released from rest follows a line only if the line is straight','Use the picture to compare fields: closer lines mean a stronger field. Lines run from high potential to low potential.','For +4q and −q, if 4n lines leave +4q then n of them end on −q, and 3n go to infinity.'),
F('Electric field',1,'Ring and arc','ring of charge Q and radius R, on the axis at x: E = {kQx;(R² + x²)^(3/2)} along the axis · E = 0 at the centre; far away E = {kQ;x²} · maximum at x = {R;√2}: E(max) = {2kQ;3√3 R²} ≈ 0.385 {kQ;R²} · arc of angle 2α at its centre: E = {2kλ sinα;R} = {kQ sinα;αR²} · semicircle: E = {2kλ;R} = {2kQ;πR²} · ring: potential at centre {kQ;R} · hollow hemispherical shell (σ): at the centre of its flat face E = {σ;4ε₀} along the axis, away from the shell, and V = {kQ;R}','Use symmetry: only the component along the axis survives, so E = ∫dE cosθ with cosθ = x/√(R² + x²). For an arc the perpendicular components cancel, and the length of the chord (2R sinα) replaces the circumference.','Ring Q = 10 nC, R = 0.1 m, x = 0.1 m: E = 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁸ × 0.1 ÷ (0.02)^(3/2) = 3182 N/C. The maximum on the axis is 2 × 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁸ ÷ (3√3 × 0.01) = 3464 N/C.','ringAxis,arcCentre,hemi'),
F('Electric field',1,'Disc, sheet and plates','disc of radius R and surface density σ on the axis: E = {σ;2ε₀}(1 − {x;√(x² + R²)}) · infinite sheet: E = {σ;2ε₀}, the same at all distances · two sheets of +σ and −σ: E = {σ;ε₀} between, 0 outside · sheets σ₁ and σ₂ (same sign): between E = {σ₁ − σ₂;2ε₀}, outside {σ₁ + σ₂;2ε₀} · just outside a conductor E = {σ;ε₀}','Add the sheet fields as vectors with directions: away from a positive sheet, towards a negative one. The field of an infinite sheet does not depend on the distance; a disc behaves like a sheet when x ≪ R.','σ = 2 μC/m²: sheet E = 2 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ (2 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 1.13 × 10⁵ N/C. A disc with R = 0.3 m at x = 0.4 m: E = 1.13 × 10⁵ × (1 − 0.4/0.5) = 2.26 × 10⁴ N/C.','discAxis,sheet,plates'),
F('Electric field',1,'Line charge and rod','infinite line of charge λ: E = {λ;2πε₀r} = {2kλ;r}, directed away from the line · finite rod, point at distance r from the line of the rod: E⊥ = {kλ;r}(sinθ₁ + sinθ₂), where θ₁ and θ₂ are the angles from the perpendicular to the ends · on the axis of a rod of length L, at distance d from the near end: E = {kQ;d(d + L)} · infinite rod: θ₁ = θ₂ = 90°','Take a small element dq = λ dx, write its field and integrate with the angle as the variable. For the infinite line Gauss’s law with a cylinder gives the result faster.','λ = 3 nC/m at r = 0.6 m: E = 2 × 9 × 10⁹ × 3 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.6 = 90 N/C. A rod of 4 nC and length 0.2 m, point on its axis 0.1 m from the near end: E = 9 × 10⁹ × 4 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (0.1 × 0.3) = 1200 N/C.','rodSide,rodAxis'),
F('Electric field',1,'Charged particle in a uniform field','a = {qE;m} in the direction of E⃗ for +q · from rest through distance d: v = √{2qEd;m} = √{2qV;m} · entering at right angles to E with speed v₀: y = {qEx²;2mv₀²}, parabola · deflection angle tanθ = {qEL;mv₀²} for plates of length L · work done W = qEd = ΔK · Millikan’s oil drop in balance: qE = mg, so q = {mg;E} (always a whole number of e) · time in the plates t = L/v₀','Treat it like projectile motion: constant acceleration along E, constant speed across it. Neglect gravity for electrons and ions unless the problem includes the weight.','An electron with v₀ = 2 × 10⁷ m/s enters plates 5 cm long with E = 2000 N/C: y = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2000 × 0.05² ÷ (2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × (2 × 10⁷)²) = 1.1 × 10⁻³ m. An oil drop of 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ kg in E = 6 × 10⁵ N/C balances with q = 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 10 ÷ (6 × 10⁵) = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, one electron.','defl'),
F('Electric field',2,'Small oscillations of a charge near a ring or a pair of charges','charge −q (mass m) displaced a small distance x along the axis of a ring of charge Q (radius R): E ≈ {kQx;R³}, so F = −{kQq;R³}x and T = 2π√{4πε₀mR³;Qq} · charge −q on the bisector of two fixed charges Q at (±a, 0), displaced a small y along the bisector: F ≈ −{2kQqy;a³}, T = 2π√{ma³;2kQq} · the moving charge must be opposite in sign to the fixed ones, otherwise it is pushed away','Expand the field for a small displacement (x ≪ R, so (R² + x²)^(3/2) ≈ R³), write F = −(constant)x and read ω² = constant ÷ m.','Ring of 10 nC, R = 0.1 m, a particle of charge −1 nC and mass 1 mg: ω² = 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁸ × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (10⁻³ × 10⁻⁶) = 90 s⁻², T = 2π ÷ √90 = 0.66 s.'),
F('Electric dipole',1,'Field of a dipole','dipole moment p = q(2a), from −q to +q, in C m · axial point (distance r from the centre): E = {2kp;r³} for r ≫ a, exact {2kpr;(r² − a²)²}, along p · equatorial point: E = {kp;r³} for r ≫ a, exact {kp;(r² + a²)^(3/2)}, opposite to p · at angle θ from the axis: E = {kp;r³}√(1 + 3cos²θ), tanα = {tanθ;2} (α from the radial line) · E ∝ 1/r³ (faster than a point charge) · E(axial) = 2 E(equatorial) at the same distance, for r ≫ a only','Find the distance r from the centre of the dipole and whether the point is on the axis or on the equatorial line. For any other angle resolve p into a component along the line and one perpendicular to it.','q = 2 μC, 2a = 2 mm so p = 4 × 10⁻⁹ C m. At r = 0.2 m: axial E = 2 × 9 × 10⁹ × 4 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.008 = 9000 N/C and equatorial E = 4500 N/C.','dipole'),
F('Electric dipole',1,'Dipole in an electric field: torque and energy','torque τ⃗ = p⃗ × E⃗, τ = pE sinθ, maximum pE at θ = 90° · potential energy U = −p⃗·E⃗ = −pE cosθ (minimum −pE at θ = 0, maximum +pE at θ = 180°, stable and unstable equilibrium) · work to turn from θ₁ to θ₂: W = pE(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂) · net force zero in a uniform field; in a non-uniform field F = p {dE;dx} · small oscillation about θ = 0: T = 2π√{I;pE} · potential of a dipole V = {kp cosθ;r²}, zero on the equatorial plane','Take θ as the angle between p⃗ and E⃗ and write U = −pE cosθ. The dipole turns to line up with E⃗ and oscillates in SHM if disturbed a little.','p = 4 × 10⁻⁹ C m in E = 5000 N/C at 30°: τ = 4 × 10⁻⁹ × 5000 × 0.5 = 10⁻⁵ N m. Turning it from 0° to 90° costs W = pE = 2 × 10⁻⁵ J.','dipole'),
F('Gauss’s law',1,'Electric flux and Gauss’s law','flux Φ = ∮E⃗·dA⃗ (N m²/C); uniform field through a plane: Φ = EA cosθ · Gauss’s law: Φ = {q(in);ε₀} for a closed surface · only the charge inside enters Φ, but E on the surface is due to all charges · the flux through a closed surface is zero if no charge is inside (or if E is uniform) · flux from a charge q through a surface subtending solid angle Ω: Φ = {q;ε₀}{Ω;4π} · a hemisphere of radius R in a uniform field along its axis: Φ = πR²E','Choose a closed (Gaussian) surface through the point where E is wanted, with the same symmetry as the charge, so that E is constant on it and perpendicular or parallel to it. Then E × area = q(in) ÷ ε₀.','A uniform field of 500 N/C at 60° to the normal through a plate of 0.2 m²: Φ = 500 × 0.2 × cos60° = 50 N m²/C. A closed surface enclosing +3 nC and −1 nC: Φ = 2 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 226 N m²/C.','flux'),
F('Gauss’s law',2,'Flux through a cube, a disc and a surface','charge q at the centre of a cube: {q;6ε₀} through each face · q at a corner: {q;8ε₀} through the cube; the three faces meeting at the corner get 0, each of the other three {q;24ε₀} · q at the centre of a face: {q;2ε₀} through the cube · q on an edge: {q;4ε₀} · charge q on the axis of a disc of radius R at distance h: Φ = {q;2ε₀}(1 − {h;√(h² + R²)}) · charge q at the centre of a sphere: all of {q;ε₀} through it, whatever the shape','Use symmetry: imagine the charge placed at the centre of a larger cube made of identical cubes (8 for a corner, 4 for an edge, 2 for a face), then divide q/ε₀ by that number.','q = 5.31 nC at the centre of a cube: each face has 5.31 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (6 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 100 N m²/C. With q at a corner, each far face has 5.31 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (24 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 25 N m²/C.'),
F('Gauss’s law',1,'Spheres and shells','conducting sphere or thin shell (charge Q, radius R): E = 0 for r < R, E = {kQ;r²} for r ≥ R, at the surface {kQ;R²} = {σ;ε₀} · uniformly charged insulating (solid) sphere: E = {kQr;R³} = {ρr;3ε₀} inside, {kQ;r²} outside; E is largest at r = R · at r = R/2 inside E is half its surface value; at r = 2R it is a quarter · several shells: E at r = k × (charge inside r) ÷ r²','Draw a spherical Gaussian surface of radius r and find the charge inside it. For a solid sphere that charge is Q(r/R)³; for a shell it is zero if r < R.','Q = 8 μC, R = 0.2 m (solid insulating): at r = 0.1 m E = 9 × 10⁹ × 8 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.1 ÷ 0.2³ = 9 × 10⁵ N/C; at r = R it is 1.8 × 10⁶ N/C; at r = 0.4 m it is 4.5 × 10⁵ N/C.','solidSph,gaussE'),
F('Gauss’s law',1,'Cylinder and slab','long charged line or cylinder surface (λ per length): E = {λ;2πε₀r} outside · uniformly charged solid cylinder (ρ, radius R): E = {ρr;2ε₀} inside, {ρR²;2ε₀r} outside · infinite plane sheet: {σ;2ε₀} · thick slab of density ρ and thickness d: inside at distance x from the middle plane E = {ρx;ε₀}, outside E = {ρd;2ε₀} · hollow cylinder: E = 0 inside','Use a coaxial cylinder (for line and cylinder) or a pillbox (for sheets and slabs) as the Gaussian surface. For the pillbox, flux comes out through both ends.','A slab of ρ = 4 × 10⁻⁸ C/m³ and d = 0.2 m: outside E = 4 × 10⁻⁸ × 0.2 ÷ (2 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 452 N/C; at x = 0.05 m from the middle plane E = 4 × 10⁻⁸ × 0.05 ÷ (8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 226 N/C.','cylHollow'),
F('Gauss’s law',1,'Non-uniform charge density','charge inside radius r: q(r) = ∫ 4πr²ρ dr, then E = {kq(r);r²} · ρ = Ar (r ≤ R): q(r) = πAr⁴, E = {Ar²;4ε₀} inside (a parabola), total charge Q = πAR⁴ · ρ = ρ₀(1 − r/R): E = {ρ₀;ε₀}({r;3} − {r²;4R}) inside, maximum {ρ₀R;9ε₀} at r = 2R/3, total Q = {πρ₀R³;3} · outside: E = kQ/r² as for a point charge · a uniform field inside a uniformly charged region would need ρ ∝ 1/r','Integrate shell by shell: a thin shell of radius r and thickness dr carries 4πr²ρ dr. Then apply Gauss’s law with a sphere of radius r. Check that E is continuous at r = R.','ρ = Ar with A = 2 × 10⁻⁶ C/m⁴ at r = 0.1 m: E = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.01 ÷ (4 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 565 N/C.'),
F('Gauss’s law',1,'Cavity in a charged sphere','sphere of uniform density ρ with a spherical cavity: the field inside the cavity is uniform, E⃗ = {ρ d⃗;3ε₀}, where d⃗ points from the centre of the sphere to the centre of the cavity · for a long cylinder with a cylindrical cavity E⃗ = {ρ d⃗;2ε₀} · method: the cavity = solid sphere of +ρ plus a sphere of −ρ · outside the sphere the field is that of the sphere without cavity minus that of the missing charge','Replace the cavity by two superposed spheres of density +ρ and −ρ. Inside the cavity E⃗ = ρr⃗₁/3ε₀ − ρr⃗₂/3ε₀ = ρ(r⃗₁ − r⃗₂)/3ε₀ = ρd⃗/3ε₀, which does not depend on the position in the cavity.','ρ = 5 × 10⁻⁶ C/m³ and d = 4 cm: E = 5 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.04 ÷ (3 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹²) = 7530 N/C, the same at every point of the cavity.'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Potential, field and the relation between them','V = {W;q₀} = work per unit charge to bring it from infinity · point charge V = {kq;r} (with sign) · ΔV = V(B) − V(A) = −∫E⃗·dl⃗ · E = −{dV;dr}, E(x) = −{∂V;∂x} etc.; E⃗ = −∇V · in a uniform field V(A) − V(B) = Ed along E; E points towards lower V · V = J/C = volt; E in V/m = N/C · 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J','To find E from V(x, y, z) differentiate with respect to each coordinate and put in the signs; to find V from E integrate along any path (the work is path independent).','V = 2x² + 3y volt: E(x) = −4x, E(y) = −3; at x = 1 m, E = (−4, −3) V/m, and |E| = √(16 + 9) = 5 V/m.'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Potential of charge distributions','V = k Σ {qᵢ;rᵢ}, an algebraic sum (a scalar) · ring on the axis: V = {kQ;√(R² + x²)}, at the centre {kQ;R} (also for any arc or polygon of equal distance) · disc on the axis V = {σ;2ε₀}(√(x² + R²) − x) · dipole V = {kp cosθ;r²}, zero on the equatorial plane · long line: ΔV = V(r₁) − V(r₂) = {λ;2πε₀} ln{r₂;r₁} · infinite sheet: V decreases by {σ;2ε₀} per metre','Add the potentials of all the charges with their signs, which is simpler than adding fields. For a continuous charge use dV = k dq/r.','A ring of 10 nC and R = 0.1 m at x = 0.1 m: V = 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁸ ÷ √0.02 = 636 V. The axial point of a dipole of p = 4 × 10⁻⁹ C m, r = 0.2 m: V = 900 V. A line of 3 nC/m: V(0.3) − V(0.6) = 2 × 9 × 10⁹ × 3 × 10⁻⁹ × ln 2 = 37.4 V.'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Potential of a sphere and shells','conducting sphere or shell: V = {kQ;r} for r ≥ R, constant {kQ;R} for r ≤ R · uniformly charged insulating sphere: V = {kQ(3R² − r²);2R³} inside; at the centre {3kQ;2R} (1.5 times the surface value) · system of concentric shells: V(r) = k Σ {Qᵢ;max(r, Rᵢ)} · inside a shell the potential is constant but the field is zero','For a point inside a set of shells, charges on shells of radius smaller than r count as kQ/r and those on larger shells count as kQ/R. Sum all terms.','Q = 8 μC, R = 0.2 m: shell V = 9 × 10⁹ × 8 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 0.2 = 3.6 × 10⁵ V. Insulating sphere: centre 5.4 × 10⁵ V; r = 0.1 m gives 4.95 × 10⁵ V. Shells of +4 nC (0.1 m) and −2 nC (0.2 m): at r = 0.15 m V = 9 × 10⁹ × (4 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.15 − 2 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.2) = 150 V.','shellV'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Potential energy of a system of charges','U = {kq₁q₂;r} for two charges (zero at infinity; negative for unlike charges) · U = k Σ {qᵢqⱼ;rᵢⱼ} over all pairs, each pair once · work done by an external agent to assemble the system = U · W(A→B) = q(V(B) − V(A)), independent of the path · three equal charges on a triangle of side a: {3kq²;a} · four equal charges on a square: (4 + √2){kq²;a}','List every pair, find its distance and add the pair energies with their signs. For N identical charges the number of pairs is N(N − 1)/2.','Four charges of 2 μC at the corners of a square of side 0.3 m: U = (4 + 1.414) × 9 × 10⁹ × (2 × 10⁻⁶)² ÷ 0.3 = 0.650 J.'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Work-energy theorem for charges','qΔV = ΔK · accelerated through V: v = √{2qV;m}; an electron through 100 V gets 5.93 × 10⁶ m/s, and 100 eV · closest approach of a charge q to a fixed charge Q (head-on): K = {kQq;r(min)} · two free particles released from r: ½m₁v₁² + ½m₂v₂² = {kq₁q₂;r} with m₁v₁ = m₂v₂ · escape to infinity needs K ≥ {kq₁q₂;r} (like charges) · the speed is largest where V (times q) is least','Conserve energy: K + U is constant, with U = qV. For two free bodies also conserve momentum. At the turning point the speed is zero (closest approach).','A 5 MeV alpha particle (2e) heading for gold (79e): r(min) = 9 × 10⁹ × 79 × 2 × (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)² ÷ (5 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹³) = 4.55 × 10⁻¹⁴ m. Two 1 g particles with 2 μC each released at 0.2 m: each moves at v = √(9 × 10⁹ × 4 × 10⁻¹² ÷ (10⁻³ × 0.2)) = 13.4 m/s.'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Equipotential surfaces and graphs of E and V','an equipotential surface has the same V everywhere; no work to move a charge on it · E⃗ is perpendicular to it and points to lower V; the surfaces never cross · a point charge gives concentric spheres; a uniform field gives parallel planes, spaced d = ΔV/E; the equatorial plane of a dipole is at 0 V · closer surfaces mean a stronger field · slope of V–x graph = −E; area under E–x graph = −ΔV · V(A) − V(B) = ∫E dx','Read E from the slope of a V–x graph (with the minus sign), and V from the area under an E–x graph. The zero of E on a graph is where V has an extreme value.','Planes of 10 V and 20 V are 5 cm apart in a uniform field: E = 10 ÷ 0.05 = 200 V/m. If E = 200 V/m up to 0.3 m and 100 V/m from 0.3 m to 0.5 m, V falls by 60 + 20 = 80 V over 0.5 m.'),
F('Potential and potential energy',1,'Self energy of a sphere or shell','conducting sphere or thin shell (Q, R): U = {kQ²;2R} = {Q²;8πε₀R} · uniformly charged solid insulating sphere: U = {3kQ²;5R} · energy stored in the field outside a sphere, from R to ∞: {kQ²;2R}; inside the solid sphere: {kQ²;10R} · expanding a charged shell from R₁ to R₂ lowers U by {kQ²;2}({1;R₁} − {1;R₂}), the work done by the electric force','Use u = ½ε₀E² integrated over the volume (shell element 4πr² dr) or build up the charge shell by shell with dW = V dq.','A shell of 8 μC and R = 0.2 m: U = 9 × 10⁹ × (8 × 10⁻⁶)² ÷ (2 × 0.2) = 1.44 J. The same charge uniformly in a solid sphere: U = 3 × 9 × 10⁹ × (8 × 10⁻⁶)² ÷ (5 × 0.2) = 1.73 J.'),
F('Conductors',1,'Conductors in electrostatic equilibrium','E = 0 inside; V is the same at every point, so the surface is an equipotential · excess charge lies only on the outer surface · E⃗ at the surface is perpendicular to it, E = {σ;ε₀} · charge density is larger where the surface is sharper (σ ∝ 1/R for spheres at the same V): corona discharge, lightning conductor · electrostatic shielding: the field in an empty cavity of a conductor is zero · outward pressure on the surface P = {σ²;2ε₀} = ½ε₀E²','Use these facts as the starting rules: the potential of a conductor is one number, and its charge is on the outside. For the pressure on a charged soap bubble or a conducting sphere use P = ½ε₀E².','A sphere of radius 0.1 m and charge 8 nC: E at the surface = 9 × 10⁹ × 8 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.01 = 7200 N/C, and the outward pressure ½ε₀E² = 0.5 × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 7200² = 2.3 × 10⁻⁴ Pa.'),
F('Conductors',1,'Induced charges and shells with charges inside','charge q placed inside a cavity of a conducting shell: induced −q on the inner surface and +q more on the outer surface, whatever the position of q · shell of charge Q: outer surface carries Q + q · for a charged sphere (a, charge q) inside a shell of inner radius b and outer radius c with charge Q: V(sphere) = k({q;a} − {q;b} + {q + Q;c}), V(shell) = {k(q + Q);c} · V(sphere) − V(shell) = kq({1;a} − {1;b}) does not depend on Q · earthing the shell removes its outer charge: V(shell) = 0','Add the induced charges layer by layer using Gauss’s law inside the metal (E = 0 there). Then find the potential by adding contributions from each surface. Earthing means V = 0, not Q = 0.','a = 0.1 m, b = 0.2 m, c = 0.3 m, q = 5 nC on the inner sphere and Q = 3 nC on the shell: outer surface 8 nC, V(shell) = 9 × 10⁹ × 8 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.3 = 240 V, V(sphere) = 240 + 9 × 10⁹ × 5 × 10⁻⁹ × (10 − 5) = 465 V. If the shell is earthed: V(sphere) = 225 V.'),
F('Conductors',1,'Spheres joined by a wire; charge sharing','two conducting spheres far apart, joined by a wire: same potential, V = {k(Q₁ + Q₂);R₁ + R₂} · q₁ : q₂ = R₁ : R₂ · σ₁ : σ₂ = R₂ : R₁ (the smaller sphere has the larger surface density and field) · energy lost as heat: U(i) − U(f) with U = kq²/2R for each sphere · identical spheres share charge equally on touching · joining two capacitors of capacitance 4πε₀R is the same problem','Total charge is conserved. Equal potential means kq₁/R₁ = kq₂/R₂. Compute the energies before and after to get the heat lost.','A 0.1 m sphere with 6 μC joined to an uncharged 0.2 m sphere: V = 9 × 10⁹ × 6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 0.3 = 1.8 × 10⁵ V; charges 2 μC and 4 μC. Energy changes from 1.62 J to 0.18 + 0.36 = 0.54 J, so 1.08 J is lost.','share'),
F('Conductors',2,'Earthing and induced charge on an earthed sphere','an earthed conductor is at V = 0 · a charged sphere (a) inside an earthed shell (b) induces −q on the shell’s inner surface, none outside: V(sphere) = kq({1;a} − {1;b}) · joining the inner sphere to the shell with a wire puts all charge on the outer surface · earthed sphere of radius R with a point charge q at a distance d from its centre: induced charge Q′ = −{qR;d} (put V at the centre = 0: kQ′/R + kq/d = 0) · the potential at the centre of a conductor is the sum of the contributions of all charges, including the far ones','Write the potential at a convenient point (usually the centre) as a sum of all contributions and set it equal to the given V (0 for earth). The charge on a surface far from the others is found from this single equation.','An earthed sphere of R = 0.1 m and a point charge of +2 μC at 0.5 m from its centre: induced charge = −2 × 0.1 ÷ 0.5 = −0.4 μC.'),
F('Capacitance and combinations',1,'Capacitance and units','C = {Q;V}, Q the charge on the positive plate and V the potential difference between the plates · unit farad (1 F = 1 C/V); 1 μF = 10⁻⁶ F, 1 pF = 10⁻¹² F · C depends only on the size, shape, spacing and the medium; not on Q or V · isolated sphere of radius R: C = 4πε₀R = {R;k} · the Earth: C = 4πε₀ × 6.4 × 10⁶ m = 711 μF · dimension M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A²','Find the potential difference that a charge Q produces between the conductors (use Gauss’s law); C = Q/V is then constant. For a single conductor the second plate is at infinity.','A sphere of radius 9 cm in air: C = 0.09 ÷ (9 × 10⁹) = 10⁻¹¹ F = 10 pF. At 1000 V it holds Q = 10 nC.'),
F('Capacitance and combinations',1,'Parallel plate capacitor','C = {ε₀A;d} (with a dielectric K filling the gap: C = {Kε₀A;d}) · E = {σ;ε₀} = {Q;ε₀A} = {V;d} between the plates · V = {Qd;ε₀A} · valid for d ≪ √A (edge effect neglected) · C ∝ A, C ∝ 1/d · charge Q on the positive plate; the inner faces carry ±Q, the outer faces of an isolated pair carry no charge','Take the area of overlap and the separation in metres. A change of d, A or K changes C in the same proportion. Plate charges follow Q = CV.','A = 100 cm² = 10⁻² m² and d = 1 mm: C = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 10⁻² ÷ 10⁻³ = 88.5 pF. At 100 V: Q = 8.85 nC and E = 100 ÷ 10⁻³ = 10⁵ V/m.','ppc'),
F('Capacitance and combinations',1,'Spherical and cylindrical capacitors','spherical: inner radius a, outer radius b, outer shell earthed: C = {4πε₀ab;b − a} = {ab;k(b − a)} · inner sphere earthed and outer shell charged: C = {4πε₀b²;b − a} (the sphere capacitor in parallel with the isolated shell 4πε₀b) · b → ∞: C = 4πε₀a · cylindrical (length L ≫ b): C = {2πε₀L;ln(b/a)}, per metre {2πε₀;ln(b/a)} · for a ≈ b the spherical capacitor is a parallel plate capacitor of area 4πa² and gap b − a','Find V between the surfaces from V = kq(1/a − 1/b) for spheres, or from the line-charge field for cylinders, and use C = Q/V. Check which conductor is earthed.','a = 0.1 m, b = 0.2 m: C = 0.1 × 0.2 ÷ (9 × 10⁹ × 0.1) = 22.2 pF. If the inner sphere is earthed instead: C = 4π × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.04 ÷ 0.1 = 44.5 pF. Cylinder a = 1 mm, b = 10 mm: C per metre = 5.56 × 10⁻¹¹ ÷ ln 10 = 24.1 pF/m.','sphcap'),
F('Capacitance and combinations',1,'Series and parallel combinations','parallel: C = C₁ + C₂ + …, same V, charge divides Q₁ : Q₂ = C₁ : C₂ · series: {1;C} = {1;C₁} + {1;C₂} + …, same Q, voltage divides V₁ : V₂ = C₂ : C₁ (V₁ = V {C₂;C₁ + C₂}) · two in series: {C₁C₂;C₁ + C₂} · n identical: parallel nC, series C/n · energy in series ∝ 1/C, in parallel ∝ C · balanced bridge ({C₁;C₂} = {C₃;C₄}): the middle capacitor has no charge','Join the points of equal potential (a wire joins them), then simplify step by step. In series every capacitor has the same charge; in parallel every one has the same voltage. Find the total, then work backwards.','2 μF and 3 μF in series across 100 V: C = 6 ÷ 5 = 1.2 μF, Q = 120 μC on each, and V = 60 V on the 2 μF and 40 V on the 3 μF.','capNet'),
F('Capacitance and combinations',1,'Parallel plates: charge on each face','a stack of isolated parallel conducting plates with charges Q₁, Q₂, … (equal areas, close together): each of the two outer faces carries {ΣQ;2} · each inner face then follows plate by plate: face on the right = plate charge − face on the left · facing surfaces of neighbouring plates are equal and opposite · field in a gap = (charge on the facing surface) ÷ (ε₀A)','Find the total charge, put half on each outer face, then work plate by plate from one end, because E must be zero inside every metal plate.','Plates of +6 μC and +2 μC: outer faces 4 μC each; plate 1 has 4 μC on its left face and 2 μC on its right face; plate 2 has −2 μC on its left face and 4 μC on its right face (sum 2 μC). The gap field is 2 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ (ε₀A).'),
F('Energy and dielectrics',1,'Energy stored and energy density','U = ½CV² = {Q²;2C} = ½QV · energy density u = {U;volume} = ½ε₀E² in a vacuum, ½Kε₀E² = {ε E²;2} in a dielectric · U = u × Ad for a parallel plate capacitor · a battery supplies QV = CV² while charging a capacitor: half is stored, the other half is lost as heat in the resistance, whatever its value · energy in series: U ∝ 1/C; in parallel: U ∝ C','Pick the form of U that contains the quantity that stays constant (V for a battery, Q for an isolated capacitor). The loss of energy on charging is always ½CV².','4 μF at 200 V: U = ½ × 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 200² = 0.08 J, the battery gave 0.16 J and 0.08 J was lost as heat. A field of 10⁵ V/m in air stores u = ½ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 10¹⁰ = 0.0443 J/m³.'),
F('Energy and dielectrics',1,'Dielectric between the plates','C = KC₀ (K = ε/ε₀ > 1) · with Q constant (battery removed): V = {V₀;K}, E = {E₀;K}, U = {U₀;K} · with V constant (battery connected): Q = KQ₀, E = E₀, U = KU₀ · induced charge on the dielectric surface Q′ = Q(1 − {1;K}) · for a conductor K → ∞ and E = 0 inside · breakdown: V(max) = E(max) d, dielectric strength E(max) · polarisation reduces the field inside','First decide what is held constant: Q (battery off) or V (battery on). Then change C by the factor K and find Q, V, E and U from Q = CV.','An air capacitor charged to 300 V and disconnected is filled with oil, K = 3: V = 100 V and E falls to 1/3. With Q = 3 μC the induced charge is Q′ = 3 × (1 − 1/3) = 2 μC.'),
F('Energy and dielectrics',1,'Battery connected or disconnected','battery connected: V constant; Q = CV ∝ C; U = ½CV² ∝ C; E = V/d; work done by the battery = VΔQ = V²ΔC; ΔU = ½V²ΔC, so half the battery’s work goes to the field and the other half to mechanical work · battery disconnected: Q constant; V = Q/C ∝ 1/C; U = Q²/2C ∝ 1/C; E = σ/ε₀ does not change with d · work done by an external agent = ΔU (disconnected)','Write which of Q and V stays fixed, then express U, E and the work in terms of the changing C. Energy balance: W(battery) = ΔU + W(mechanical).','C₀ = 10 μF at 100 V: U₀ = 0.05 J. Fill with K = 4 with the battery on: Q = 4 mC, U = 0.2 J, the battery gives 3 × 10⁻³ × 100 = 0.3 J, so 0.15 J is mechanical work. Battery off: U = 0.05 ÷ 4 = 0.0125 J.'),
F('Energy and dielectrics',1,'Dielectric or conducting slab between the plates','slab of thickness t and constant K in a gap d (plate area A): C = {ε₀A;d − t + t/K} · conducting slab (K → ∞): C = {ε₀A;d − t}, independent of the position of the slab · slab fills the gap: C = {Kε₀A;d} · for constant Q: V = E₀(d − t) + {E₀t;K} · the slab can be shifted between the plates without changing C','Treat the layers (air, slab, air) as capacitors in series, or equivalently take the effective gap d − t + t/K. A thin metal sheet of thickness t reduces the gap by t.','A = 0.01 m², d = 5 mm, t = 3 mm of K = 3: d − t + t/K = 2 + 1 = 3 mm, so C = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.01 ÷ 0.003 = 29.5 pF. With a metal slab: 2 mm gives 44.3 pF.','capDiel'),
F('Energy and dielectrics',1,'Layers and side-by-side dielectrics','layers of thickness dᵢ and constants Kᵢ across the full area (series): C = {ε₀A;Σ dᵢ/Kᵢ} · regions of area Aᵢ side by side (parallel): C = {ε₀ΣKᵢAᵢ;d} · slab of constant K pushed a length x into plates of length L and width w: C = {ε₀w(L + (K − 1)x);d} · force pulling the slab in (battery on): F = ½V² {dC;dx} = {ε₀w(K − 1)V²;2d} (independent of x) · the field in layer i is E₀/Kᵢ if the same flux density passes through every layer','Decide if the dielectrics are stacked along the field (add dᵢ/Kᵢ) or side by side (add Kᵢ Aᵢ/d). Mixed cases are series and parallel combinations of the pieces.','A = 0.01 m²; layers 2 mm (K = 2) and 3 mm (K = 6): Σd/K = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 mm, C = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.01 ÷ (1.5 × 10⁻³) = 59 pF. Two halves with K = 2 and K = 4 side by side, d = 1 mm: C = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × (2 × 0.005 + 4 × 0.005) ÷ 10⁻³ = 265 pF.'),
F('Energy and dielectrics',2,'Force between the plates','F = {Q²;2ε₀A} = ½QE = ½ε₀AE² = {ε₀AV²;2d²} · each plate feels the field of the other plate, {σ;2ε₀}, which is half the field between them · pressure on the plates {σ²;2ε₀} = ½ε₀E² · work to increase the separation by Δd with Q constant: W = {Q²Δd;2ε₀A} · with the battery connected F ∝ 1/d²','Use half of the field between the plates when finding the force on one plate. Then work = force × displacement for small changes, or the energy change for large ones.','A = 0.01 m², d = 1 mm, V = 1000 V: E = 10⁶ V/m, F = ½ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.01 × (10⁶)² = 0.0443 N.'),
F('Joining, charging and circuits',1,'Joining charged capacitors and the heat loss','plates of the same sign joined: V = {C₁V₁ + C₂V₂;C₁ + C₂} · opposite plates joined: V = {C₁V₁ − C₂V₂;C₁ + C₂} (sign of the larger charge) · loss of energy (same polarity) ΔU = {C₁C₂(V₁ − V₂)²;2(C₁ + C₂)}; opposite polarity {C₁C₂(V₁ + V₂)²;2(C₁ + C₂)} · a charged capacitor joined to an uncharged one of equal C loses half its energy · charge is conserved; energy goes as heat and radiation, independent of the resistance','Conserve the charge on the joined plates, put the two capacitors at the same final V, and find the energy before and after. Take care of the sign for opposite connection.','2 μF at 100 V joined to 3 μF at 50 V (same polarity): V = (200 + 150) ÷ 5 = 70 V; loss = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁻⁶ × 50² ÷ (2 × 5 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.5 × 10⁻³ J. With opposite polarity V = (200 − 150) ÷ 5 = 10 V.'),
F('Joining, charging and circuits',1,'Charging and discharging through a resistor','charging: q = CV(1 − e^(−t/RC)), I = {V;R} e^(−t/RC), V(C) = V(1 − e^(−t/RC)) · discharging: q = Q₀e^(−t/RC), I = I₀e^(−t/RC) · time constant τ = RC: at t = τ the charging capacitor has 63% of its final charge, a discharging one 37% · half-life t½ = RC ln 2 = 0.693 RC · current is greatest at t = 0 · energy dissipated while charging = ½CV²','At t = 0 an uncharged capacitor behaves as a wire, and after a long time every capacitor branch carries no current. For other times use the exponentials with τ = RC.','R = 1 kΩ, C = 100 μF, V = 10 V: τ = 0.1 s, final charge 1 mC. At t = 0.1 s: q = 1 × (1 − e⁻¹) = 0.632 mC and I = (10 ÷ 1000) × e⁻¹ = 3.68 mA.','rcg'),
F('Joining, charging and circuits',1,'Steady-state circuits with capacitors','in a steady state no current flows in a branch with a capacitor: remove it, solve the resistor circuit and take V(C) as the potential difference between the capacitor’s end points · a capacitor in parallel with a resistor carries that resistor’s voltage · just after a switch is closed an uncharged capacitor is a wire; a charged one is a battery of emf Q/C · when a switch changes the connections, apply conservation of charge on each isolated pair of plates · energy supplied by a source = emf × charge passed','Redraw the circuit with the capacitors removed, find the currents with Ohm’s law, then put the voltages across the capacitors from the potentials at their ends. Q = CV for each capacitor.','10 V battery with 3 Ω and 2 Ω in series; a 5 μF capacitor across the 2 Ω resistor: I = 10 ÷ 5 = 2 A, V(C) = 4 V, Q = 5 × 4 = 20 μC.')
],
look:['F = kq₁q₂/r², k = 9 × 10⁹ N m²/C², E = kq/r², F = qE','Dipole: axial {2kp;r³}, equatorial {kp;r³}, τ = pE sinθ, U = −pE cosθ','Gauss: Φ = {q(in);ε₀}; sphere E = {kQ;r²}; line {λ;2πε₀r}; sheet {σ;2ε₀}','Solid sphere inside E = {kQr;R³}; shell inside E = 0; V = {kQ;R} inside','V = {kq;r}, U = {kq₁q₂;r}, E = −{dV;dr}, W = qΔV, K = qV','Ring axis: E = {kQx;(R² + x²)^(3/2)}, V = {kQ;√(R² + x²)}','C = {Q;V}; plates {ε₀A;d}; sphere 4πε₀R; spherical {4πε₀ab;b − a}','Series {1;C} = Σ{1;Cᵢ}; parallel C = ΣCᵢ; U = ½CV² = {Q²;2C}','Slab: C = {ε₀A;d − t + t/K}; u = ½ε₀E²; force between plates {Q²;2ε₀A}','Joining: V = {C₁V₁ + C₂V₂;C₁ + C₂}, loss {C₁C₂(V₁ − V₂)²;2(C₁ + C₂)}; RC: q = Q(1 − e^(−t/RC))'],
notes:[
{h:'Charge and Coulomb’s law',items:['q = ne with e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C; charge is conserved and quantised.','k = 9 × 10⁹ N m²/C²; the force in a medium is divided by K.','Like charges repel, unlike charges attract; the forces form an action-reaction pair.','A third charge is in equilibrium at x = d√q₁/(√q₁ + √q₂) from q₁ (between like charges).','Balls on strings: tanθ = F/mg; the same angle in a liquid gives K = ρ/(ρ − σ).','Superposition: add forces as vectors, never their magnitudes.']},
{h:'Electric field',items:['E = kq/r² away from +q; force on −q is opposite to E.','Field of a ring on its axis is zero at the centre and largest at x = R/√2.','Infinite sheet σ/2ε₀ (no distance dependence); two opposite sheets σ/ε₀ between and 0 outside.','Infinite line λ/(2πε₀r) falls as 1/r.','A charge in a uniform field moves like a projectile: a = qE/m.','Field lines never cross, start on + and end on −, and leave conductors at 90°.']},
{h:'Electric dipole',items:['p = q × 2a points from − to +.','For r ≫ a, the axial field 2kp/r³ (along p) is twice the equatorial field kp/r³ (opposite to p).','Torque pE sinθ; energy −pE cosθ; stable at θ = 0 and unstable at θ = 180°.','Work to rotate from θ₁ to θ₂: pE(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂).','No net force in a uniform field; in a non-uniform field the dipole is pulled to the stronger field.','Potential of a dipole kp cosθ/r²; zero on the equatorial plane.'],fig:'dipole'},
{h:'Gauss’s law',items:['Φ = q(in)/ε₀; only the enclosed charge counts in the flux, but all charges set E.','Shell: E = 0 inside and kQ/r² outside; solid sphere: kQr/R³ inside.','Cube: q at the centre gives q/6ε₀ per face; at a corner q/8ε₀ through the cube.','Slab: E = ρx/ε₀ inside at distance x from the middle plane.','For ρ = Ar, E = Ar²/4ε₀ inside; for a cavity E = ρd/3ε₀, uniform.','Choose a Gaussian surface in which E is constant and perpendicular or parallel to the surface.']},
{h:'Potential and potential energy',items:['V = kq/r is a scalar; add potentials with signs.','E = −dV/dr; E points towards lower V; equipotentials are perpendicular to E.','U = kq₁q₂/r; for a system add all pairs once.','Shell: constant V = kQ/R inside; solid insulating sphere has 1.5 kQ/R at the centre.','Work by the field = −ΔU = q(V(A) − V(B)); path independent.','Closest approach: all the kinetic energy becomes kQq/r.'],fig:'shellV'},
{h:'Conductors',items:['E = 0 and V = constant inside a conductor; charge is on the outer surface.','E = σ/ε₀ at the surface; pressure σ²/2ε₀; σ ∝ 1/R for spheres at the same V.','q inside a cavity induces −q inside and +q outside the shell.','Spheres joined by a wire: same V; q₁ : q₂ = R₁ : R₂; heat is lost.','Earthing sets V = 0; the charge left follows from the potential, not from Q = 0.','Shielding: the field inside a closed hollow conductor is zero.'],fig:'share'},
{h:'Capacitance and combinations',items:['C = Q/V depends only on geometry and medium.','Parallel plate ε₀A/d; sphere 4πε₀R; spherical 4πε₀ab/(b − a); cylinder 2πε₀L/ln(b/a).','Series: same Q and 1/C adds; parallel: same V and C adds.','With the inner sphere earthed, C = 4πε₀b²/(b − a).','Charge on the outer plates of a stack follows from conservation of charge.','n identical capacitors: series C/n, parallel nC.'],fig:'capNet'},
{h:'Energy and dielectrics',items:['U = ½CV² = Q²/2C = ½QV; u = ½ε₀E².','Battery on: V constant, Q ∝ C, U ∝ C; battery off: Q constant, U ∝ 1/C.','Dielectric K: C → KC, E → E/K, induced charge Q(1 − 1/K).','Slab: C = ε₀A/(d − t + t/K); a conducting slab removes t from the gap.','Layers add dᵢ/Kᵢ; side-by-side regions add KᵢAᵢ.','Force between plates Q²/2ε₀A; a slab is pulled in with F = ε₀w(K − 1)V²/2d.'],fig:'capDiel'},
{h:'Joining, charging and circuits',items:['After joining: V = ΣCV/ΣC (same polarity); the loss is C₁C₂(V₁ − V₂)²/2(C₁ + C₂).','Charging a capacitor from a battery: half the energy is lost as heat.','τ = RC; the charge reaches 63% in one time constant; discharging falls to 37%.','Steady state: no current through a capacitor; remove it and find its voltage from the resistor circuit.','At t = 0 an uncharged capacitor is a short circuit; at t → ∞ it is an open circuit.','Check the units: F = C/V, J = ½CV², s = RC.']}],
asked:[
{t:'Coulomb force and equilibrium of charges',what:'Two or three point charges lie on a line or at the corners of a figure. Find the force on one of them, the position of equilibrium or the third charge needed.',steps:['Draw the force of each charge on the one in question.','Add as vectors; use symmetry to cancel components.','For equilibrium equate the two forces and use x = d√q₁/(√q₁ + √q₂).'],ex:'+4 μC and +9 μC, 30 cm apart: the zero-force point is 12 cm from the 4 μC.',tp:'charge'},
{t:'Charged balls on strings, in air and in a liquid',what:'Two charged balls hang apart. Find the angle, the charge or the dielectric constant of the liquid in which the angle stays the same.',steps:['Balance mg, tension and the Coulomb force: tanθ = F/mg.','In the liquid divide F by K and reduce the weight by the upthrust.','For the same angle K = ρ/(ρ − σ).'],ex:'Balls of density 1600 kg/m³ in oil of 800 kg/m³: K = 2.',tp:'charge'},
{t:'Electric field of point charges and the neutral point',what:'Charges are placed at points on a line, on a square or on a triangle. Find the field at a point, where the field is zero, or where it is greatest.',steps:['Find each field from kq/r² with its direction.','Resolve into components and add.','For a zero use the opposite directions; for a maximum differentiate.'],ex:'Two charges of 2 μC at (±0.1 m, 0): on the bisector at y = 0.1 m E = 1.27 × 10⁶ N/C.',tp:'field'},
{t:'Field of a ring, a disc, a rod or an arc',what:'A charged ring, disc, rod or semicircle is given. Find the field on its axis or at its centre.',steps:['Use the symmetry to keep one component.','Take dq and integrate, or use the standard result.','Check the limits: far away it is kq/x², near the centre of a ring zero.'],ex:'Ring of 10 nC, R = 0.1 m, at x = 0.1 m: E = 3182 N/C.',tp:'field'},
{t:'Charged particle in a uniform field',what:'An electron, proton or ion enters or is released in a uniform field between plates. Find its acceleration, speed, deflection or time.',steps:['a = qE/m, direction from the sign of the charge.','Along the field use v² = u² + 2as; across it the speed is constant.','Use the energy relation qEd = ΔK when only the speed is asked.'],ex:'An electron entering at 2 × 10⁷ m/s in 2000 N/C over 5 cm is deflected 1.1 mm.',tp:'field'},
{t:'Electric dipole: field, torque and energy',what:'A dipole of given moment is placed in a field or at a point near another charge. Find the field at a point, the torque, the energy or the work to turn it.',steps:['Axial 2kp/r³, equatorial kp/r³ (opposite directions).','τ = pE sinθ, U = −pE cosθ.','Work = pE(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂); oscillation T = 2π√(I/pE).'],ex:'p = 4 × 10⁻⁹ C m in 5000 N/C at 30°: τ = 10⁻⁵ N m.',tp:'dipole',fig:'dipole'},
{t:'Electric flux and Gauss’s law',what:'A charge is inside or near a cube, a sphere or another closed surface. Find the flux through the surface or through a part of it.',steps:['Φ = q(in)/ε₀ for a closed surface.','Use symmetry: cube faces, 8 cubes at a corner, 4 at an edge.','Flux through a plane in a uniform field is EA cosθ.'],ex:'q at a corner of a cube: q/24ε₀ through each far face.',tp:'gauss'},
{t:'Field of spheres, shells, cylinders and sheets',what:'A uniformly charged sphere, shell, cylinder, wire or plane is given. Find the field at several distances and compare them.',steps:['Choose the Gaussian surface of the same shape.','Find q inside the surface: for a solid sphere Q(r/R)³.','Write the field and compare the ratios.'],ex:'Solid sphere Q = 8 μC, R = 0.2 m: E(r = 0.1) = 9 × 10⁵ N/C, E(R) = 1.8 × 10⁶ N/C.',tp:'gauss',fig:'gaussE'},
{t:'Non-uniform charge density and cavity',what:'The density depends on r (ρ = Ar or ρ₀(1 − r/R)) or a sphere has a spherical hole. Find the field or the position where it is maximum.',steps:['Integrate 4πr²ρ dr to get q(r).','E = kq(r)/r²; differentiate for the maximum.','For a cavity: E = ρd/3ε₀, uniform and along d.'],ex:'ρ = Ar: E = Ar²/4ε₀ inside; with A = 2 × 10⁻⁶, r = 0.1 m: 565 N/C.',tp:'gauss'},
{t:'Potential of charges, rings and shells',what:'Charges, a ring or a charged conducting sphere are given. Find the potential at a point or the potential difference between two points.',steps:['V = kq/r for each charge; add with signs.','Inside a shell V = kQ/R; on the ring axis kQ/√(R² + x²).','For a solid insulating sphere V(centre) = 1.5 kQ/R.'],ex:'Shells of +4 nC (0.1 m) and −2 nC (0.2 m): at r = 0.15 m, V = 150 V.',tp:'pot',fig:'shellV'},
{t:'Potential energy of a system and work done',what:'Several charges are assembled, moved or released. Find the energy of the arrangement, the work needed, or the speed gained.',steps:['U = Σ kqᵢqⱼ/rᵢⱼ over all pairs.','Work by the agent = ΔU = qΔV.','Conserve energy (and momentum for free particles).'],ex:'Four 2 μC charges on a square of 0.3 m side: U = 0.650 J.',tp:'pe'},
{t:'Equipotentials, E from V and graphs',what:'V(x, y, z) or a V–x or E–x graph is given. Find E, the direction of the field, or V at some point.',steps:['E = −dV/dx for each coordinate.','Read slope for E and area for ΔV.','E is perpendicular to the equipotential, towards lower V.'],ex:'V = 2x² + 3y: at x = 1, E = (−4, −3), |E| = 5 V/m.',tp:'pe'},
{t:'Conducting spheres, shells and charge sharing',what:'Spheres or shells are connected by wires, earthed, or have charges inside cavities. Find charges, potentials or the heat lost.',steps:['Use conservation of charge and equal potential.','Induced charges: −q inside, +q outside the shell.','Earthing: set V = 0 to find the charge.'],ex:'6 μC on a 0.1 m sphere joined to an uncharged 0.2 m sphere: 2 μC and 4 μC; 1.08 J lost.',tp:'pot',fig:'share'},
{t:'Capacitance of geometry and combinations',what:'A capacitor of a given shape or a network of capacitors is given. Find the capacitance between two points, the charge or the voltage on one of them.',steps:['Parallel plate ε₀A/d, sphere 4πε₀R, spherical 4πε₀ab/(b − a).','Simplify series and parallel groups step by step.','Q is the same in series and V is the same in parallel.'],ex:'2 μF and 3 μF in series across 100 V: 60 V and 40 V.',tp:'cap',fig:'capNet'},
{t:'Dielectric slab, conducting slab and layers',what:'A slab or several layers fill the gap of a parallel plate capacitor. Find C, the field, the charge or the energy, with the battery on or off.',steps:['C = ε₀A/(d − t + t/K); layers: Σdᵢ/Kᵢ.','Say what stays constant: V (battery on) or Q (battery off).','Find the energy and the work done on the slab.'],ex:'A = 0.01 m², d = 5 mm, slab 3 mm of K = 3: C = 29.5 pF.',tp:'diel',fig:'capDiel'},
{t:'Energy, force and heat loss in capacitors',what:'A capacitor is charged, its plates are pulled apart, or two capacitors are joined. Find the energy stored, the force or the heat produced.',steps:['U = ½CV² or Q²/2C, as the case requires.','Force between plates Q²/2ε₀A.','Joined: conserve charge and find the common V.'],ex:'2 μF at 100 V joined to 3 μF at 50 V: V = 70 V, loss 1.5 mJ.',tp:'diel'},
{t:'Charging, discharging and steady-state circuits',what:'A capacitor is in a circuit with resistors. Find the charge or current at a time, or the charge once a steady state has been reached.',steps:['Steady state: no current through the capacitor.','Voltage across it = potential difference of its ends.','Time dependence: q = Q(1 − e^(−t/RC)) or Qe^(−t/RC).'],ex:'R = 1 kΩ, C = 100 μF, 10 V: at t = RC the charge is 0.632 mC.',tp:'cap'}],
guide:{
intro:'Electrostatics (with capacitors) is one of the most important Class 12 chapters in JEE Mains: it usually gives 2 to 4 questions every year, almost always from capacitors (series, parallel, dielectrics, energy), Gauss’s law with spheres and shells, potential and potential energy of charges, and the dipole. The formulas are short; the marks come from choosing the correct symmetry and from sign care. About 14 hours is enough.',
plan:[
['Day 1 · 90 min','Charge, Coulomb’s law, superposition, the equilibrium of charges and charged balls on strings.'],
['Day 2 · 2 hours','Electric field of point charges, ring, disc, rod and sheet; the motion of a charge in a uniform field.'],
['Day 3 · 90 min','The dipole: field at axial and equatorial points, torque, energy and oscillations.'],
['Day 4 · 2 hours','Flux and Gauss’s law: sphere, shell, cylinder, slab, non-uniform density and the cavity.'],
['Day 5 · 2 hours','Potential, potential energy of a system, E = −dV/dx, equipotentials and the work-energy method.'],
['Day 6 · 90 min','Conductors: shells, induced charges, connected spheres, earthing.'],
['Day 7 · 2 hours','Capacitors: C of the standard shapes, series and parallel, dielectric and conducting slabs, battery on or off.'],
['Day 8 · 90 min','Joining capacitors, heat loss, RC charging and steady-state circuits; take a mock test and read the Revise tab.']],
basics:['Vectors and their components (for the net force and the net field).','Calculus: integration of simple functions (for dq = λ dx or ρ 4πr² dr).','Work, energy and the work-energy theorem.','Kinematics in a plane (for a charge in a uniform field).','Ohm’s law and series circuits (for the capacitor circuits).','Solid angle and symmetry ideas.'],
path:[
{t:'Step 1 · Coulomb’s law and charge (Day 1)',d:'Learn the force law, superposition, the equilibrium position and the charged balls on strings.',ready:'you can find the equilibrium point of a charge between two others in under a minute.',go:'formulas'},
{t:'Step 2 · Electric field and the dipole (Days 2 and 3)',d:'Learn the field of the standard bodies and the dipole results.',ready:'you can write the axial and equatorial dipole fields and the torque without looking.',go:'formulas'},
{t:'Step 3 · Gauss’s law (Day 4)',d:'Learn the Gaussian surface for each symmetry and the field graphs of spheres and shells.',ready:'you can sketch E against r for a solid sphere, a shell and a cavity.',go:'asked'},
{t:'Step 4 · Potential and energy (Day 5)',d:'Learn V for points, rings and shells and the energy of a system of charges.',ready:'you can find the energy of four charges at the corners of a square.',go:'formulas'},
{t:'Step 5 · Conductors (Day 6)',d:'Learn induced charges, shells with charges inside and charge sharing between spheres.',ready:'you can find all the charges and the potentials of a sphere inside a shell.',go:'asked'},
{t:'Step 6 · Capacitors (Days 7 and 8)',d:'Learn the shapes, the combinations, the dielectric slab and the energy rules.',ready:'you can say what is constant and how U, Q, V and E change when a slab is inserted.',go:'asked'},
{t:'Step 7 · Practise, then test (Day 8)',d:'Practise by question type, then take the mock test.',ready:'you score 8 out of 10 in practice twice in a row.',go:'practice'}],
think:['Decide first: field or potential? Field is a vector, potential a scalar.','If the charge has a symmetry (sphere, cylinder, plane), use Gauss’s law.','For several charges draw the field vectors and add them, or add the potentials with signs.','For energy, count every pair of charges once.','For a conductor, E = 0 inside and V is constant; use conservation of charge.','For capacitors, write down which of Q and V stays constant before you change anything.','Check the units and the order of magnitude; the factor 9 × 10⁹ is easy to drop.','MCQs have negative marking: remove two wrong options before guessing.'],
watch:['A field is a vector: add components, not magnitudes.','Potential is a scalar: add with signs; the field can be zero where the potential is not zero, and the other way round.','The field inside a shell is zero, the potential is not.','The energy of a system counts each pair once; do not double it.','In a medium, divide Coulomb’s force and the field by K; capacitance is multiplied by K.','With the battery connected V is constant; with the battery removed Q is constant.','The force on a plate uses half of the field between the plates.','In RC charging the exponent is −t/RC; use 1 − e^(−t/RC) for charge on a charging capacitor.'],
myths:[
['Myth: The field is zero where the potential is zero.','Truth: On the equatorial plane of a dipole the potential is zero but the field is not; at the centre of a ring the field is zero but the potential is not.'],
['Myth: The flux through a closed surface depends on the charges outside.','Truth: The net flux depends only on the charge inside; outside charges add as much flux going in as going out.'],
['Myth: Gauss’s law gives the field of any charge distribution.','Truth: It is useful only when the field has a symmetry that makes E constant on the surface; it is always true, but not always easy.'],
['Myth: A charge released from rest always moves along a field line.','Truth: It follows a field line only if the line is straight; otherwise its velocity and the line are different.'],
['Myth: The electric field inside a charged shell is zero because there is no charge in it.','Truth: Zero field inside comes from symmetry: the field of the shell charge cancels at every point of the hollow.'],
['Myth: A dielectric always lowers the energy of a capacitor.','Truth: With the battery connected the energy goes up by the factor K; it goes down only when the charge is fixed.'],
['Myth: Energy is lost only when there is a resistor of large value.','Truth: Half the battery’s energy is lost in charging whatever the resistance; it is the heat produced by the current.'],
['Myth: The capacitance of a capacitor depends on its charge and voltage.','Truth: It depends only on the geometry and the medium; Q and V change together in C = Q/V.'],
['Myth: A conducting slab in a capacitor has no effect on the capacitance.','Truth: It shortens the gap by its thickness t (C = ε₀A/(d − t)), though the position of the slab does not matter.'],
['Myth: In series capacitors the voltage divides equally.','Truth: The charge is equal; the voltage is larger across the smaller capacitor (V ∝ 1/C).']]},
extra:[]
};
})();
